1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,273
<font color="#FFFFFF">Diffusion models produce stunning samples, but they
are slow,</font>

2
00:00:04,353 --> 00:00:09,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">often needing a thousand sequential denoising steps
to generate a single sample.</font>

3
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:13,875
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper from Stanford asks a different question
than most prior work.</font>

4
00:00:13,955 --> 00:00:18,297
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of cutting the number of steps and paying
with sample quality,</font>

5
00:00:18,377 --> 00:00:21,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">can we keep every step but run them in parallel,</font>

6
00:00:21,409 --> 00:00:23,855
<font color="#FFFFFF">trading extra compute for lower latency?</font>

7
00:00:23,935 --> 00:00:26,382
<font color="#FFFFFF">The answer is a method called ParaDiGMS,</font>

8
00:00:26,462 --> 00:00:33,583
<font color="#FFFFFF">which uses Picard iterations to guess the whole
denoising trajectory and refine it in parallel until it converges.</font>

9
00:00:33,663 --> 00:00:38,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">It delivers two-to-four times faster sampling with
no measurable drop in quality.</font>

10
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:43,111
<font color="#FFFFFF">The central limitation of diffusion models is
sampling speed.</font>

11
00:00:43,191 --> 00:00:49,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">A standard denoising diffusion probabilistic model
can take a thousand sequential passes</font>

12
00:00:49,122 --> 00:00:52,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">through the neural network to create one sample,</font>

13
00:00:52,357 --> 00:00:55,108
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is far too slow for interactive use.</font>

14
00:00:55,188 --> 00:01:00,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">The popular fix is to simply use fewer denoising
steps, as DDIM and DPMSolver do,</font>

15
00:01:00,647 --> 00:01:04,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">but reducing steps comes at the cost of sample quality.</font>

16
00:01:04,354 --> 00:01:08,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">The field has largely accepted this
quality-for-speed tradeoff.</font>

17
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors pursue an orthogonal direction.</font>

18
00:01:11,709 --> 00:01:14,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of trading quality for speed,</font>

19
00:01:14,126 --> 00:01:17,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">they ask whether we can trade compute for speed.</font>

20
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:21,297
<font color="#FFFFFF">The goal is to lower the latency of generating a
single sample,</font>

21
00:01:21,377 --> 00:01:24,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">not just the throughput of generating many.</font>

22
00:01:24,186 --> 00:01:26,131
<font color="#FFFFFF">At first this seems impossible,</font>

23
00:01:26,211 --> 00:01:31,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">because denoising is inherently sequential: each
step depends on the previous one.</font>

24
00:01:31,568 --> 00:01:35,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">Naive parallelization can generate multiple samples
at once,</font>

25
00:01:35,487 --> 00:01:40,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">but making a single sample appear faster in
wall-clock time is a much harder problem.</font>

26
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:43,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key contribution is ParaDiGMS,</font>

27
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:47,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">short for Parallel Diffusion Generative Model Sampling.</font>

28
00:01:47,521 --> 00:01:53,343
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is the first general method that lets you spend
extra parallel compute to sample a</font>

29
00:01:53,423 --> 00:01:55,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">pretrained diffusion model faster,</font>

30
00:01:55,784 --> 00:01:57,302
<font color="#FFFFFF">without any retraining.</font>

31
00:01:57,382 --> 00:02:00,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, it is orthogonal to existing techniques,</font>

32
00:02:00,923 --> 00:02:06,746
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it can be layered on top of DDIM or DPMSolver to
yield ParaDDIM and ParaDPMSolver,</font>

33
00:02:06,826 --> 00:02:08,899
<font color="#FFFFFF">combining both axes of speedup.</font>

34
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:12,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is also compatible with classifier-free guidance.</font>

35
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:15,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method builds on Picard iterations,</font>

36
00:02:15,394 --> 00:02:21,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">a classic technique for solving ordinary
differential equations by fixed-point iteration.</font>

37
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:28,607
<font color="#FFFFFF">The insight is to write the value at each timestep
as the initial value plus the integral of the drift along the path.</font>

38
00:02:28,687 --> 00:02:31,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">Starting from a full guess of the trajectory,</font>

39
00:02:31,577 --> 00:02:36,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">ParaDiGMS updates every timestep simultaneously
using the cumulative drift,</font>

40
00:02:36,393 --> 00:02:39,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">and repeats until the values stop changing.</font>

41
00:02:39,154 --> 00:02:43,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because each iteration can be computed in parallel
across timesteps,</font>

42
00:02:43,521 --> 00:02:48,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the number of iterations to converge is much
smaller than the number of steps,</font>

43
00:02:48,787 --> 00:02:51,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">the whole trajectory resolves much faster.</font>

44
00:02:51,484 --> 00:02:56,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">In practice it uses a sliding window of a fixed
size to respect GPU memory,</font>

45
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:00,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">advancing the window as soon as the earliest
timesteps converge.</font>

46
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:05,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments cover two very different domains to
show generality.</font>

47
00:03:05,616 --> 00:03:11,668
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the robotics side, the authors test diffusion
policies on Robosuite Square, PushT,</font>

48
00:03:11,748 --> 00:03:12,966
<font color="#FFFFFF">and FrankaKitchen,</font>

49
00:03:13,046 --> 00:03:17,872
<font color="#FFFFFF">measuring task reward averaged over hundreds of
evaluation episodes.</font>

50
00:03:17,952 --> 00:03:19,171
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the image side,</font>

51
00:03:19,251 --> 00:03:25,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">they evaluate StableDiffusion version 2 generating
768-by-768 images from COCO captions,</font>

52
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:30,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">judged by CLIP score, and pixel-space LSUN Church
and Bedroom models,</font>

53
00:03:30,577 --> 00:03:31,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">judged by FID score.</font>

54
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:38,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these span latent-space and pixel-space
diffusion at very different scales.</font>

55
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:45,883
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is remarkably consistent: a
two-to-four times speedup across every task and every sampler,</font>

56
00:03:45,963 --> 00:03:48,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no measurable degradation in quality.</font>

57
00:03:48,832 --> 00:03:50,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">On robotic control,</font>

58
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:56,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">ParaDDPM speeds up sampling by roughly 3.4 to 3.9
times while holding task reward constant.</font>

59
00:03:56,347 --> 00:03:58,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">On StableDiffusion version 2,</font>

60
00:03:58,328 --> 00:04:03,782
<font color="#FFFFFF">it brings the time to generate an image down from
fifty seconds to under fifteen,</font>

61
00:04:03,862 --> 00:04:08,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">a 3.4x gain, and stacking it on faster samplers
reaches four times.</font>

62
00:04:08,439 --> 00:04:14,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">The number of parallel iterations needed for
convergence is up to twenty times smaller</font>

63
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:16,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">than the number of sequential steps,</font>

64
00:04:16,774 --> 00:04:18,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is why the approach works.</font>

65
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:25,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main ablation studies the effect of the error
tolerance in the fixed-point iteration.</font>

66
00:04:25,237 --> 00:04:30,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">A lower tolerance means more iterations and slower
sampling but higher fidelity,</font>

67
00:04:30,609 --> 00:04:32,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">while a looser tolerance is faster.</font>

68
00:04:32,959 --> 00:04:40,266
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper shows there is a comfortable regime where
a fairly relaxed tolerance still preserves sample quality.</font>

69
00:04:40,346 --> 00:04:42,146
<font color="#FFFFFF">Importantly, on LSUN Church,</font>

70
00:04:42,226 --> 00:04:46,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">ParaDDPM matches full DDPM's FID score at nearly
four times the speed,</font>

71
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:51,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas simply reducing DDIM to 500 steps produces
visibly worse images,</font>

72
00:04:51,762 --> 00:04:57,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">demonstrating that the gains genuinely come from
parallelism rather than fewer steps.</font>

73
00:04:57,770 --> 00:05:03,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">To put concrete numbers on it: sampling speedups of
two to four times across the board.</font>

74
00:05:03,370 --> 00:05:09,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">StableDiffusion version 2 drops from fifty seconds
to fourteen-point-six seconds per image.</font>

75
00:05:09,228 --> 00:05:14,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">On LSUN Church, FID barely moves, from
twelve-point-eight to twelve-point-nine,</font>

76
00:05:14,313 --> 00:05:16,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">while running almost four times faster.</font>

77
00:05:16,823 --> 00:05:22,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the deep reason it scales is that the number of
parallel iterations to converge is</font>

78
00:05:22,359 --> 00:05:26,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">up to twenty times smaller than the thousand
sequential steps.</font>

79
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:31,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting takeaway is a new axis for accelerating
diffusion models.</font>

80
00:05:31,197 --> 00:05:34,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rather than sacrificing quality by taking fewer steps,</font>

81
00:05:34,755 --> 00:05:38,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">ParaDiGMS spends parallel compute to run all the
steps faster,</font>

82
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:43,373
<font color="#FFFFFF">cutting sampling latency by two to four times with
no loss in quality,</font>

83
00:05:43,453 --> 00:05:47,195
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it composes with the fast samplers people already use.</font>

84
00:05:47,275 --> 00:05:50,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">Looking forward, as parallel hardware keeps improving,</font>

85
00:05:50,833 --> 00:05:55,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">sampling time will be limited only by the small
number of Picard iterations,</font>

86
00:05:55,841 --> 00:05:58,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">pointing toward even faster real-time generation.</font>
